{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Let me gulp down.</b> I open my mouth and you pour much into it, as we learned: one does not stuff a camel but one does feed it by pouring — מַלְעִיטִין." "<b>Of the red, the red stuff.</b> Red lentils. And that day Abraham died, so that he would not see Esau his grandson going out to a bad way of life — and that is not a good old age that the Holy One, blessed be He, had promised him. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, shortened five years from his years: Isaac lived 180 years and this one [Abraham] 175. And Jacob cooked lentils to feed the mourner. And why lentils? Because they are round like a wheel, for mourning is a wheel that turns in the world. Furthermore, lentils have no mouth — so the mourner has no mouth, for he is forbidden to speak. Therefore the custom is to feed the mourner at the start of his meal with eggs, which are round and have no mouth — so the mourner has no mouth, as they say in Mo'ed Katan (Mo'ed Katan 21b): a mourner all three first days does not return greeting to anyone, and certainly does not initiate; from three to seven he returns but does not initiate, and so on. (In old Rashi.)"], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:00:34.095735844Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}